# Rule-trigger items: the harness prefixes "First read the whole file <read> with the Read tool. Then answer: ...". After the restructure each `read` should load the matching .claude/rules/<rule>.md; before it, the answer comes from CLAUDE.md. Graded like recall.
{"id": "rule-faces-separation", "rule": "faces", "read": "photosearch/face_verify.py", "prompt": "In verify-face-labels, what is separation(P, Q), and why is it a percentile rather than min()?", "any": [["5th percentile", "p5", "percentile"], ["min\\(\\)", "minimum", "one bad label", "drags", "disable"]], "facts": "separation(P, Q) is a low percentile (the 5th percentile, SEPARATION_PERCENTILE = 5) of the distances between the persons' face sets, not min(). One bad label would drag a min() to zero and silently disable the pair; the percentile tolerates a few bad labels."}
{"id": "rule-llm-unusable-answer", "rule": "llm-passes", "read": "photosearch/describe.py", "prompt": "What does the worker do when describe.UnusableAnswer is raised for a photo, and how does that differ from a transport timeout?", "any": [["failure row", "failures", "index_errors", "one attempt", "attempt spent"], ["defer", "omitted", "transient", "transport"]], "facts": "describe.UnusableAnswer is a bad-output failure: the worker reports it as a failure row (FailedResult in the `failures` list), which spends one attempt and logs an index_errors row, nothing written. A transport timeout is transient: it is omitted/deferred and does not spend an attempt."}
{"id": "rule-ingest-faces-done", "rule": "ingest", "read": "photosearch/batch_state.py", "prompt": "Which worker pass counts an exhausted photo with no output toward `done` instead of `failed`, and why is that only safe for that pass?", "expect": ["faces"], "any": [["nobody", "no face", "no detectable", "empty.*(result|output)"]]}
{"id": "rule-maintenance-stacking-incremental", "rule": "maintenance", "read": "photosearch/maintenance.py", "prompt": "Is the maintenance sweep's stacking stage a full re-detect or missing-only? What ledger makes that possible and what can it never do?", "any": [["stacking_seen", "incremental", "missing-only", "dirty"], ["never.*(wipe|clear)", "clear_stacks", "unscoped"]], "facts": "The sweep's stacking stage is missing-only (incremental), via stacking.run_incremental_stacking and the stacking_seen ledger (only dirty photos are re-evaluated; it writes a diff). It must never call clear_stacks() or an unscoped detect_stacks/run_stacking, and never pass photo_ids=[] (read as the whole library)."}
{"id": "rule-workers-failures-list", "rule": "workers", "read": "photosearch/worker.py", "prompt": "When processing a photo raises a non-transient exception in a worker pass, what does the worker send to the server for it, and what does the server do with that?", "any": [["failures", "FailedResult", "failure row", "_failure_row"], ["one attempt", "attempt", "index_errors"]]}
{"id": "rule-video-probe-owner", "rule": "video", "read": "photosearch/video_probe.py", "prompt": "Which machine runs the video probe and scan, and what is the NAS's role for video?", "any": [["worker", "desktop", "native"], ["never.*(decode|probe)", "stores", "serves", "Range", "walks"]]}
{"id": "rule-print-topaz-exit-code", "rule": "print", "read": "photosearch/upscale.py", "prompt": "How does the Topaz upscale integration decide that a run succeeded, and why not the exit code?", "any": [["output file", "file appear", "new file", "file shows up", "exists"], ["\\b9\\b", "127", "wrong", "non-zero", "exit code"]], "facts": "Success is decided by an output file having appeared, not by the exit code: a good Topaz run exits 9 under WSL interop (never 0), and the broken tpai.exe exits -1 and writes nothing. So never judge by exit code and never use check=True."}
{"id": "rule-frontend-ps-poll", "rule": "frontend", "read": "frontend/dist/shared.js", "prompt": "What does PS.poll do differently from setInterval, and what incident motivated it?", "expect": ["setInterval"], "any": [["settle", "after.*resolve", "previous", "re-arm", "hidden"], ["SMB", "2026-09-19", "40", "starv", "disk"]], "facts": "PS.poll re-arms only after the previous call's promise settles (and pauses while the tab is hidden), whereas setInterval fires regardless of whether the last request returned. Motivated by an incident where setInterval polling on a starved NAS (SMB/disk) stacked requests and filled all ~40 request threads, wedging the status page and the box it reported on."}
{"id": "rule-search-compose-scope", "rule": "search", "read": "photosearch/search.py", "prompt": "What does _compose_scope in search_combined do, and which three filter kinds does it fold into the id scope?", "expect": ["scope"], "any": [["date", "camera", "people|person"]]}
